  <div class="project-identity">
    <h2 class="paper-title" id="project-title"><span class="paper-project"><b class="paper-brand">Mind2Act</b> World:</span><span class="paper-subtitle"><span>Evaluating Reasoning–Acting Coordination</span><span>in Robotic Manipulation</span></span></h2>
  </div>
  <figure class="intro-film"><video controls loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster="media/posters/CP04__CP04_easy.jpg"><source src="media/videos/CP04__CP04_easy.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video><figcaption>Project film · demonstration placeholder</figcaption></figure>
<div id="introduction" class="research-abstract" aria-label="Project introduction">
<p>Toward general-purpose embodied intelligence, robots must execute long-horizon tasks in complex, dynamic environments, combine high-level task organization with physical interaction, and continually adapt their decisions as the environment changes. However, existing robotic manipulation benchmarks often focus on isolated atomic skills or short-horizon objectives in simplified scenes. Such settings are useful for evaluating primitive control and conducting reproducible experiments, but offer limited evidence of whether robots can maintain task state throughout a multi-stage manipulation process and effectively couple reasoning with physical execution.</p>
<p>To address this gap, we introduce <strong class="research-emphasis">Mind2Act World</strong>, a benchmark that systematically evaluates robotic task reasoning, physical execution, and their coordination through contextualized tasks, tiered stress configurations, and process-level diagnostics. Mind2Act World comprises 15 tasks across three complementary dimensions: <strong class="research-emphasis">Mind, Act, and Mind2Act</strong>. <strong class="research-emphasis">Mind</strong> measures the ability to organize actions at the task-cognition level, including maintaining task-relevant state, interpreting instructions and observations, inferring goals and prerequisites, and determining an appropriate sequence of operations. <strong class="research-emphasis">Act</strong> measures the ability to realize intended operations in the physical world through timely, precise, and stable interactions under timing, geometric, contact, and stability constraints. <strong class="research-emphasis">Mind2Act</strong> evaluates whether reasoning and execution can be jointly realized through ongoing interaction: task intent must guide physically valid actions, while actual execution outcomes must inform remaining goals, action timing, and phase progression. The central question is whether a robot can maintain both task-level correctness and effective physical execution while keeping subsequent decisions consistent with what has actually been completed.</p>
<figure class="intro-overview"><img src="media/images/mind2act-introduction-overview.svg" width="1200" height="820" alt="Overview of Mind2Act World: task reasoning leads to physical execution; observed physical outcomes update task state and the next decision."><figcaption>Overview of Mind2Act World</figcaption></figure>
<p>The task suite embeds primitive manipulation skills in multi-stage contexts with practical task backgrounds, persistent state, and explicit phase dependencies, with tiered stress configurations designed around each task's mechanisms. Each task has <strong class="research-emphasis">Easy, Medium, and Hard</strong> levels that vary historical information load, planning and phase dependencies, precision and contact requirements, and dynamic response windows to examine capability boundaries under different pressures. Evaluation combines task success rates with process evidence on goal selection, physical execution outcomes, and phase progression to analyze whether failures primarily reflect <strong class="research-emphasis">task understanding and planning deficiencies in Mind, physical execution deficiencies in Act, or difficulties coordinating reasoning and execution in Mind2Act</strong>. Through stratified results, process records, and applicable controlled comparisons, the benchmark aims to reveal the conditions under which models fail and how local deviations affect subsequent decisions and propagate through multi-stage manipulation.</p>
<p>To support reproducible and interpretable comparisons across <strong class="research-emphasis">VLA/WAM policies, Coding Agents, and hybrid systems</strong>, Mind2Act World specifies observation and action interfaces, timing conditions, and disclosure requirements for execution assistance, and determines task completion from actual environment-side physical states. By combining contextualized task design, tiered stress evaluation, and closed-loop process diagnostics, <strong class="research-emphasis">Mind2Act World</strong> provides a systematic framework for studying reasoning, execution, and their coordination in robotic manipulation.</p>
</div>
